          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Paris Conservatoire</strong><br />\nClaude Debussy was born on August 22, 1862, in Saint-Germain-en-Laye, France. He began piano lessons at an early age and entered the Paris Conservatoire at age ten. Here, he studied piano and composition, showcasing his unique talent and often controversial musical vision that broke from traditional forms.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Winning the Prix de Rome</strong><br />\nIn 1884, Debussy won the prestigious Prix de Rome with his composition \"L'enfant prodigue.\" This prize awarded him a scholarship to the Académie des Beaux-Arts, which included a four-year residency at the Villa Medici in Rome to advance his studies in music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Musical Style</strong><br />\nAfter his return from Rome, Debussy developed his distinct musical style characterized by a use of non-traditional scales such as the whole tone scale, and innovative chord structures. His compositions are noted for their sensory content and frequent use of chromaticism, which were revolutionary at the time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
