          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influences and Artistic Associations</strong><br />\nDebussy drew inspiration from a variety of sources including literature, art, and other musical traditions such as Russian and Asian music. His association with symbolist poets and impressionist painters influenced his approach to composition, which was akin to musical impressionism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Famous Works</strong><br />\nDebussy's most famous works include the orchestral piece \"Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune,\" the opera \"Pelléas et Mélisande,\" and his suite for solo piano \"Suite bergamasque,\" which contains the well-known \"Clair de lune.\" These works showcase his ability to evoke mood and atmosphere.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Innovation in Opera and Chamber Music</strong><br />\nDebussy's opera \"Pelléas et Mélisande\" represents a departure from traditional operatic forms and is celebrated for its subtlety and expressiveness. He also made significant contributions to chamber music, with innovative pieces like his String Quartet in G minor.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
