          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Debussy's Philosophy of Music</strong><br />\nDebussy believed music was primarily a sensuous art form and should evoke images and feelings. He often rebelled against strict compositional forms and traditional expectations, favoring free-flowing structures and harmony to communicate the nuances of feeling and atmosphere.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Musical Activities</strong><br />\nDebussy's personal life was tumultuous with two marriages and numerous affairs. Despite personal challenges, he remained an influential figure in Parisian musical and artistic circles, frequently engaging in critiques and collaborative works with other leading artists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br />\nDebussy died of cancer during World War I on March 25, 1918, in Paris. At the time of his death, France was embroiled in conflict, and his passing was somewhat overshadowed. However, posthumously, Debussy's music gained widespread recognition, and he is now regarded as one of the most important and influential composers of the early 20th century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
